  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 205 PM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Most of the forecast area is under a marginal \n(level 1 of 5) risk for severe thunderstorms (excluding locations \nsouth and east of Lillington and Smithfield), with damaging wind as \nthe threat. In addition, most of the forecast area is under a slight \n(level 2 of 4) risk for excessive rainfall (excluding the Triad) \ntoday, and most of the forecast area is under a marginal risk for \nexcessive rainfall (excluding the Triad) Saturday.\n\nWhile the radar was quiet this morning, isolated thunderstorms have \nbegun to develop along a cold front primarily to the north and west \nof the Triad as of 2:00pm. The effective shear across central North \nCarolina is between 25-30 knots, which should be enough to provide \nat least some amount of storm organization. Depending on the model, \nMLCAPE could be anywhere between 500 and 2000 J/kg. Considering the \nwind speed throughout the vertical profile are not very high, storm \nmovement will be slow, enhancing the potential for heavy rainfall to \noccur. It appears that a line of showers and thunderstorms could \ndevelop along the east side of the Appalachian Mountains with the \nfront and track southeast, passing to the south of the Triad and \nprimarily impacting southwestern portions of the forecast area. \nStill, confidence was not high enough to go above a 50% chance of \nrain in any location. Precipitation coverage is likely to peak in \nthe mid to late afternoon, and gradually decrease during the \nevening. In addition, there were some computer models indicating \nthat a heavier area of rainfall could develop northeast of the \nTriangle after sunset.\n\nThe cold front should extend from northeast to southwest across the \nforecast area by sunrise on Saturday, but will essentially drift \nover the area for the next 24 hours before finally moving offshore \nSunday. As more of the area will be on the cool side of the front, \nthe chance for showers and storms will be 10-20% less on Saturday \nthan today. Slow storm movement is the primary reason for the \nmarginal risk of excessive rainfall.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A hot start to September across central NC.\n\nAloft, the ~595 dam mid-level anticyclone will build eastward into \nand settle over the region through mid-week. Disturbances in the \nnorthern stream may help push the ridge south and west toward the \nend of the week, but uncertainty remains in that regard. At the \nsurface, a weak pressure trough may remain in place across the area \nthrough, while Bermuda high pressure ridges wwd across the region. \nExpect a sly component to the wind across much of the area through \nat least mid-week, with the potential for a trough to amplify across \nthe region late-week. Low level thicknesses will increase through \nthe middle of the week, maxing out ~1430 m on Mon to potentially \n1440+ m by Wed and Thu. Temperatures should moderate through mid-\nweek, with highs in the mid/upr 80s on Sun, increasing to mid/upr \n90s by Wed and Thu (10+ degrees above normal). Lows in the 70s \nexpected through much of the week. Heat index values of 100-105 are \npossible Tue-Thu across much of central NC, apart from the NW \nPiedmont. Given the prolonged period of heat with little relief \novernight, the WPC Experimental Heat Risk continues to show Major to \nExtreme risk of heat-related impacts across central NC, especially \nWed and Thu.",
